<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reims</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rheims, Durocortorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Reims is a city in Champagne, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Durocortorum of the Remi and later capital of the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-belgica">Belgica</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> was baptised there by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Remigius around 500, and most French kings were crowned in its cathedral, a Gothic masterpiece and World Heritage Site. On 7 May 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> signed its surrender there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reims.jpg" alt="Reims" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Crazy Tourist (thecrazytourist.com) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sub%C3%A9_Fountain,_Reims,_France.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
